diff --git a/paper-tex/arxiv-abstract.txt b/paper-tex/arxiv-abstract.txt
index 81b72de..05ad2a0 100644
--- a/paper-tex/arxiv-abstract.txt
+++ b/paper-tex/arxiv-abstract.txt
@@ -1 +1 @@
-Agentic AI systems take consequential actions governed by more than one pre-action control at once: authority, resource, and evidence gates that can admit, degrade, or remediate an action before it executes. This paper's central object is remediation-induced control coupling: a remediation applied by one control can change the action, evidence, or context another control evaluates, invalidating that control's earlier judgment. We formalize this coupling, give a remediate-and-regate composition protocol restoring per-action soundness after one remediation, and show that two implemented remediation operators (evidence substitution and resource-budget downroute) do not commute -- a finite-model checker finds non-confluent instances, so a fixed operator order is pre-registered: remediation order becomes governance semantics. A governed evidence buffer that trusts its own most recent admitted write is a further instance of the same problem at the level of state -- current admissibility does not imply future reference trustworthiness -- and is vulnerable to poisoning from declared-uncovered defect classes; two mitigations reduce, not eliminate, that exposure. We also show that for positive-weight linear aggregation of gate outcomes, a vetoed action is admitted exactly when the admission threshold does not exceed the aggregator's largest leave-one-out weight sum, and that composition adds no coverage: classes no member gate covers remain uncovered, reported honestly. Empirically, on a deterministic open-data artifact composing three published engines unmodified, over 30 pre-registered seeds and two workflows, every hypothesis is supported except one buffer-contamination robustness claim under the smaller workflow, reported as such. This is a mechanism demonstration on open payload data with a synthetic metadata layer, not a claim about production prevalence.
+Agentic AI systems take consequential actions governed by authority, resource, and evidence controls that can admit, degrade, or remediate an action. This paper's central object is remediation-induced control coupling: a remediation applied by one control can change the action, evidence, or context another control evaluates, invalidating that control's earlier judgment. We formalize this coupling and, for the current one-shot idempotent setting, give a remediate-and-regate protocol that restores per-action soundness under its stated assumptions. We also show that two implemented remediation operators (evidence substitution and resource-budget downroute) do not commute -- a finite-model checker finds non-confluent instances, so a fixed operator order is pre-registered: remediation order becomes governance semantics. A governed evidence buffer that trusts its own most recent admitted write is a further instance of the same problem at the level of state -- current admissibility does not imply future reference trustworthiness -- and is vulnerable to poisoning from declared-uncovered defect classes; two mitigations reduce, not eliminate, that exposure. We additionally show that for positive-weight linear aggregation of gate outcomes, a vetoed action is admitted exactly when the admission threshold does not exceed the aggregator's largest leave-one-out weight sum, and that composition adds no coverage: classes no member gate covers remain uncovered, reported honestly. Empirically, on a deterministic open-data artifact composing three published engines unmodified, CH1-CH5 meet their registered decision rules across all 30 pre-registered seeds; CH6 does so under W1 but not under the smaller W2 workflow, reported as such. This is a mechanism demonstration on open payload data with a synthetic metadata layer, not a claim about production prevalence.
